fe or Solid Democratic", "Prediction markets price Luján's win probability at 97-99%"], "rationale": "Polling, money, expert ratings, and markets all align: Luján leads every surveyed matchup by double digits, has vastly out-raised a poorly funded challenger, and faces no outside opposition spending. With New Mexico's Democratic lean and incumbency advantages, a mid-teens margin is the clear expectation.", "watch": "Only a late-breaking scandal or a dramatic national backlash against Democrats could pull the margin into single digits, and no current evidence points that way.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ben Ray Luján (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Ben Ray Luján seeks a second term against Republican Larry Marker in a state that has favored Democrats in federal races. Every recent poll shows Luján ahead by 16 to 18 points among likely voters, including a large October survey from Research & Polling. The incumbent has raised roughly seventy times more money than his challenger and holds an enormous cash advantage, while no outside groups have spent against him.\n\nRaters across the spectrum call the race Safe or Solid Democratic, and prediction markets give Luján a 97 to 99 percent chance of winning. New Mexico's partisan lean, a favorable national environment, and incumbency all point to a margin in the mid-teens. Marker, a little-funded first-time candidate, would need a major shift in the final weeks of the campaign to make this race competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9923, "margin": 15.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9906, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9897, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9862, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9853, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9894, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9908, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem":